  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0001 database is designed to manage a music library and user interactions with it. It contains 11 tables that work together to store information about users, artists, albums, songs, playlists, and settings. The users table stores information about each user, including their name, email, password, and preferences, with a unique identifier and timestamps for when they were created and last updated. The password_resets table is used to store temporary passwords for users who have requested a password reset. The artists table contains information about each artist, including their name and image, while the albums table stores information about each album, including the artist it belongs to. The songs table stores information about each song, including its title, length, and lyrics, and is linked to the albums and artists tables through foreign keys. The playlist_folders table stores information about each playlist folder, including its name and the user it belongs to, while the playlists table stores information about each playlist, including its name and rules, and is linked to the users and playlist_folders tables. The interactions table stores information about each user's interactions with songs, including whether they liked the song and how many times they played it, and is linked to the users and songs tables. The playlist_song table stores the relationships between playlists and songs, and the settings table stores key-value pairs of settings for the application. The personal_access_tokens table stores information about personal access tokens, including the token, the type of tokenable object, and the last time it was used.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nKoel is an open-source personal audio streaming server created to manage and stream personal music collections. Originally developed by Phan An, Koel is designed using modern web technologies such as Laravel and Vue. With Docker support, it is easy to deploy and runs smoothly on contemporary browsers. The application aims to recreate the familiar and intuitive experience of popular streaming services like Spotify and Apple Music.\n\n 2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Koel is to offer a seamless and advanced personal music streaming experience. It ensures users can upload, organize, and stream their music collections with rich features that enhance music management and playback across devices.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Individual Music Enthusiasts: People who wish to store, manage, and stream their personal music collection.\n- Developers & Contributors: Individuals contributing to the open-source project, enhancing its functionalities.\n- Administrators: Users with administrative privileges managing user access and system settings.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Music Streaming: Users can stream their music collections with support for multiple audio formats.\n- User Management: Allows registration, login/logout functionality, and multi-user support, including admin features.\n- Music Library Management: Upload, delete, and edit metadata for tracks, albums, and artists.\n- Playlist Management: Create, manage, and organize playlists with support for playlist folders and smart playlists.\n- Advanced Features: Provides features like equalizer settings, cross-device playback synchronization, and visualizers.\n- 3rd-party Integrations: Ability to integrate with other services and applications.\n- Interaction Tracking: Supports history tracking and marking favorites, plus statistics on play counts and user interactions.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The system should be responsive and fast, supporting smooth streaming and browsing.\n- Usability: The interface must be intuitive, mimicking familiar music streaming services for ease of use.\n- Compatibility: Compatible with modern browsers and should not rely on obsolete technology.\n- Security: Ensure secure access and data management, including support for password resets and personal access tokens.\n- Scalability: Capable of handling a large number of songs and users without performance degradation.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0001.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0001\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `preferences` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `password_resets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `artists` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `image` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `albums` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `artist_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `albums_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`artist_id`) REFERENCES `artists` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `songs` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `length` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lyrics` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `album_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `artist_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `songs_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`album_id`) REFERENCES `albums` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `songs_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`artist_id`) REFERENCES `artists` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `playlist_folders` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `playlist_folders_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `playlists` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `rules` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `folder_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `playlists_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `playlists_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`folder_id`) REFERENCES `playlist_folders` (`id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `interactions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `song_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `liked` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `play_count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `interactions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `interactions_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`song_id`) REFERENCES `songs` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `playlist_song` (\n  `playlist_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `song_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`playlist_id`, `song_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `playlist_song_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`playlist_id`) REFERENCES `playlists` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `playlist_song_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`song_id`) REFERENCES `songs` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `settings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  `value` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `personal_access_tokens` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `tokenable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_used_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
